When creating an AgentCore runtime by uploading a ZIP artifact from S3, the only strict requirements are:


The ZIP must contain a valid Python project.


The entryPoint must reference an importable module and callable function.


All dependencies must be declared in requirements.txt.


Beyond that, the internal code structure is flexible. You can vertically segment your agent by organizing tools into separate files or folders (for example, tools/echo.py, tools/math_add.py) and simply import and route them from your main entrypoint module. AgentCore does not require a single-file implementation — it only needs a resolvable entrypoint.
In other words, when deploying via S3:


The S3 artifact defines the runtime package.


The entrypoint defines where execution begins.


Your internal modular structure (multiple tool files, folders, layered design) is fully supported as long as it is importable.


The key takeaway is that AgentCore runtime hosting is packaging-oriented, not file-structure–restricted — modular design is both supported and recommended.
